2026-07-07 10:29:13,763 - INFO - ([0;32mTask_Analysis_Agent_round_0_02_omori_fitting_and_figures[0m) 6: Tool `analyze_image_file` returned [id: call_OWiqBJCrng0XJJOhPh0hy79f]:
[0;33mThe figure is a 3-panel comparison of p-value versus period, with shared y-axis and the same period range on the x-axis. Each panel compares two curves:

- Blue: Northern area
- Red: Southern area

Vertical dashed lines mark reference periods near 0.73 day and 1.40 day.

Compared split latitudes:
- 35.70°N
- 35.72°N
- 35.74°N

Overall behavior across split lines:

- Southern area:
  - The red curve is relatively consistent across all three split latitudes.
  - It generally rises from lower periods toward ~0.8 at longer periods.
  - The trajectory shape changes only modestly between panels, suggesting comparatively robust behavior to the choice of split latitude.

- Northern area:
  - The blue curve is much less consistent across split latitudes.
  - At short periods, it starts high in all panels, but after ~0.75–0.85 day it drops, with the magnitude of the drop depending strongly on split latitude.
  - For 35.70°N, the decline is moderate and values remain around ~0.67–0.75 at longer periods.
  - For 35.72°N, the drop is stronger, reaching roughly ~0.57–0.65.
  - For 35.74°N, the drop is strongest and remains much lower, around ~0.30–0.43 over much of the longer-period range.
  - This indicates substantial sensitivity of the northern p-value trajectory to the exact split latitude.

North–south contrast:

- In all panels, the north starts above the south at short periods.
- Near ~0.7–0.8 day, the curves converge or cross.
- Beyond that, the south is consistently above the north, and this separation becomes stronger as the split moves northward from 35.70°N to 35.74°N.

Visually obvious uncertainties / low-count behavior:

- The most obvious sign of potential instability is the abrupt step-like drop in the northern curve just after ~0.75–0.85 day, especially for 35.72°N and 35.74°N.
- The increasing divergence of the northern trajectory across very small split-latitude changes (only 0.02° increments) suggests sensitivity that may reflect low sample counts or marginal bin membership near the boundary.
- The longer-period end, especially near 1.4 day, may also be less stable because it is an edge point and is visually isolated by the right dashed line.
- By contrast, the southern curves appear smoother and more repeatable, with fewer abrupt changes, so they look less affected by split-position uncertainty.

Report-ready takeaway:
- The robustness test suggests the southern-area p-value pattern is fairly stable across nearby split latitudes, whereas the northern-area pattern is not. The main uncertainty is concentrated in the northern series beyond ~0.8 day, where small changes in split latitude produce large changes in p-value, consistent with visually apparent boundary sensitivity and possible low-count behavior.[0m

